WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.938
Hello and welcome. I'm James Murphy.

00:00:01.938 --> 00:00:06.765
In this episode,&nbsp;we're talking about the core guideline that says that you shouldn't use std::endl

00:00:06.765 --> 00:00:11.856
and some&nbsp;common pushback about this guideline that I hear concerning multi-threaded code.

00:00:11.856 --> 00:00:17.614
The core guideline&nbsp;says that most of the time you should just prefer to use a newline literal inside of your strings

00:00:17.614 --> 00:00:20.368
instead of using the standard endline.

00:00:20.368 --> 00:00:22.250
The reason for doing this is twofold.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:25.567
Sometimes it avoids an&nbsp;extra write operation.

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:29.307
Here we needed a second call to the write operator in order to write the new&nbsp;line.

00:00:29.307 --> 00:00:33.931
But here, we were able to just include it in our string since we were already using a string&nbsp;literal.

00:00:33.931 --> 00:00:37.782
But even in a case like this, where you can't save the extra write operation,

00:00:37.782 --> 00:00:41.920
it's still&nbsp;preferred to use a new line over standard endline.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.920
The reason for this is buried in the standard. So,&nbsp;don't feel bad if you didn't know where to look.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.519
But the effect of writing a standard endline

00:00:48.519 --> 00:00:54.063
is to first write the new line character and then call flush on the stream.

00:00:54.063 --> 00:00:58.089
Not all the time,&nbsp;but sometimes, flushing can be very expensive,

00:00:58.089 --> 00:01:00.433
like if you're writing to a file, especially.

00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:05.588
And in that case, doing unnecessary flushes will severely impact your performance.

00:01:05.588 --> 00:01:09.217
If you're&nbsp;not already familiar, a lot of output streams are buffered.

00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:12.269
What that means is, when you write text&nbsp;into this file object,

00:01:12.269 --> 00:01:18.400
it doesn't necessarily mean that that immediately writes the text to&nbsp;the underlying resource, in this case, the file.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.974
Instead, it may just keep track of all the stuff&nbsp;that you've written in an in-memory buffer

00:01:22.974 --> 00:01:27.087
and then sometime later write all the stuff to&nbsp;the file all at once.

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:35.520
The process of writing the in-memory buffer data of the file object into&nbsp;the actual file on disk is the process of flushing

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.901
If we were to use endline because of the flush&nbsp;that end line implies,

00:01:38.901 --> 00:01:44.354
This means that we're going to be flushing and synchronizing with the&nbsp;file system three times in this code.

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:48.710
Whereas, in this code, we don't necessarily have to flush it&nbsp;all until the file object is destroyed.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:53.573
So, in its destructor, it would call flush and flush all the&nbsp;data all at once.

00:01:53.573 --> 00:01:59.760
The actual amount of speed-up you get from doing this is going to depend&nbsp;a lot on the relative cost of doing a flush.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.367
So, for something like writing to a file, you'll&nbsp;typically see a bigger speed up.

00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:07.762
But for something like writing to standard out, you wouldn't see as&nbsp;much of a speed up.

00:02:07.762 --> 00:02:12.000
It's not required by C++, but a lot of implementations will flush the buffer

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.177
for&nbsp;standard out every time it sees a new line anyway.

00:02:15.177 --> 00:02:17.045
In that case, if you use endline,

00:02:17.045 --> 00:02:21.377
you're basically&nbsp;just doing a redundant flush of zero characters immediately afterwards.

00:02:21.377 --> 00:02:25.085
Any decent implementation&nbsp;won't actually flush if there are zero characters.

00:02:25.085 --> 00:02:28.883
So, your penalty will just be the penalty of an&nbsp;extra if-check.

00:02:28.883 --> 00:02:32.819
However, whenever I suggest this guideline, inevitably someone asks:

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:37.600
Hey, doesn't&nbsp;endline protect you in a multi-threaded situation?

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.349
I'm not quite sure where this myth originates from&nbsp;but it's sure widespread.

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:45.705
And the answer is: No, it does not protect you at all.

00:02:45.705 --> 00:02:49.600
Let's take a look at&nbsp;a simple multi-threaded example to investigate.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.571
Here, I have a worker that takes in a start, a&nbsp;stop and a stream to output to.

00:02:54.571 --> 00:02:56.930
It just loops from the start until the stop

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:01.137
and output some&nbsp;info about which thread and what number it's on.

00:03:01.137 --> 00:03:05.490
Then down here, I just start up two&nbsp;threads that call the worker function.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.438
The first worker is going to print&nbsp;from ten thousand to twenty thousand

00:03:08.438 --> 00:03:12.480
and the second worker is going to print&nbsp;from twenty thousand to thirty thousand.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.653
For this example, the stream I'm using is just&nbsp;a file stream going to this out.txt

00:03:17.653 --> 00:03:21.186
Okay, I went ahead and built it and let's run it.

00:03:21.186 --> 00:03:25.200
It appeared to&nbsp;run fine with no errors but let's check the output.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.360
When we look at the output, we see&nbsp;all kinds of messed up lines here.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.542
Some lines like this one printed out just fine.

00:03:31.542 --> 00:03:35.138
Other lines have sort of part of one line and part of another line mixed together.

00:03:35.138 --> 00:03:38.088
And other lines are&nbsp;just fragments of what they should be.

00:03:38.088 --> 00:03:41.351
So, clearly this is not the output I was expecting.

00:03:41.351 --> 00:03:44.560
So,&nbsp;what output was I realistically hoping for?

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.598
Well, I'm doing these two different chunks&nbsp;of work in two separate threads.

00:03:47.598 --> 00:03:51.840
So, I can't really expect them to be ordered in any&nbsp;particular way with respect to each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.480
However, the bare minimum that I could hope&nbsp;for is that, I at least get complete lines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:57.950
Let's take that as our goal.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:02.391
Suppose we&nbsp;don't care the order the lines come in, only that we get complete lines.

00:04:02.391 --> 00:04:04.583
So, how do we&nbsp;accomplish that?

00:04:04.583 --> 00:04:08.579
Well, as per the common myth, let's see what happens if we try standard endline.

00:04:08.579 --> 00:04:11.517
Does this protect us in some way?

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.334
Recompile

00:04:13.334 --> 00:04:14.649
Rerun

00:04:14.649 --> 00:04:17.425
But the output is exactly the same, garbage.

00:04:18.394 --> 00:04:19.769
I&nbsp;hope this helps dispell the rumor

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:23.896
that standard endline somehow is better than newline because&nbsp;it fixes data races.

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:26.319
It clearly does not do that.

00:04:26.319 --> 00:04:30.888
The underlying issue here was not whether or&nbsp;not the data has been flushed to the file yet.

00:04:30.888 --> 00:04:36.160
The real issue is that we have two threads trying&nbsp;to write to the same place at the same time.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.000
If changing a newline to an endline fixes this&nbsp;problem for you, then you got lucky.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.877
Most likely this did not fix your issue and it just made it&nbsp;less likely to occur.

00:04:46.877 --> 00:04:51.773
So, you now have the same bug that will only manifest in a very rare case.

00:04:51.773 --> 00:04:54.884
Maybe it only happens every 100th time you run the program.

00:04:54.884 --> 00:04:59.920
And honestly, to me a bug like that&nbsp;is much worse than a bug that fails everytime.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.854
At least, if it fails every time, I know something&nbsp;is wrong and can try to fix it.

00:05:03.854 --> 00:05:07.721
If endline doesn't actually fix this, then what's the real fix?

00:05:07.721 --> 00:05:12.568
Let's&nbsp;take a look at the solutions for C++ 20, C++ 17 and C++ 11.

00:05:12.568 --> 00:05:15.919
If you have the luxury of using C++ 20, it's&nbsp;really simple.

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:18.056
Start by including the syncstream header.

00:05:18.056 --> 00:05:23.600
Make a standard osyncstream that wraps the&nbsp;output stream that you actually want to write to.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:29.040
Then everywhere that you were going to write&nbsp;to the output stream, write to the osyncstream.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.763
osyncstream guarantees that it's&nbsp;safe to write to an output stream

00:05:32.763 --> 00:05:38.317
from multiple threads as long as all of those&nbsp;threads write to it through an osyncstream object.

00:05:38.317 --> 00:05:42.579
Under the hood, it uses some kind of lock or mutex&nbsp;in order to accomplish this.

00:05:42.579 --> 00:05:47.598
It will acquire the lock or mutex on construction and release it when&nbsp;it's destructed.

00:05:47.598 --> 00:05:53.047
And while we're at it, since we're using C++ 20, we might as well use jthread&nbsp;instead of thread.

00:05:53.047 --> 00:05:56.690
jthread is the same as a thread except it automatically joins in its destructor

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:59.205
so you don't have to remember to call join.

00:05:59.205 --> 00:06:05.040
And here's the output. In that case, as you can&nbsp;see, we get only complete lines in the output.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.208
Of course, these threads are computing. So, the&nbsp;order of these lines is not deterministic.

00:06:09.208 --> 00:06:11.839
If I were to run this again, I would get something&nbsp;completely different.

00:06:11.839 --> 00:06:16.338
So, the order is still not guaranteed. But at least, we don't have any mangled&nbsp;lines.

00:06:16.338 --> 00:06:19.158
Okay, what about C++ 17?

00:06:19.158 --> 00:06:24.417
osyncstream and jthread were added in C++ 20, so we need&nbsp;to use something else.

00:06:24.417 --> 00:06:26.887
osyncstream took care of the mutex for us.

00:06:26.887 --> 00:06:29.788
Prior to C++ 20, we need&nbsp;to do it ourselves.

00:06:29.788 --> 00:06:33.246
So, include mutex and make a global write_mutex.

00:06:33.246 --> 00:06:38.235
You might be tempted to just&nbsp;lock and unlock the mutex yourself and that would probably work.

00:06:38.235 --> 00:06:40.387
But that's not the best solution.

00:06:40.387 --> 00:06:44.328
The issue with this is that if an exception is thrown even if you handle it somewhere else,

00:06:44.328 --> 00:06:46.613
you&nbsp;might not release the lock in time.

00:06:46.613 --> 00:06:49.663
In C++ 17, use scoped_lock.

00:06:49.663 --> 00:06:54.110
It locks the mutex on construction&nbsp;and releases it on destruction.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:57.645
If you're using C++ 11 on the other hand, then use standard&nbsp;lock_guard.

00:06:57.645 --> 00:06:59.216
It does the same thing as scoped_lock.

00:06:59.216 --> 00:07:03.570
scoped_lock is basically just an all-around&nbsp;better version of lock_guard.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:07.075
Of course, we can't remove lock_guard from the language because&nbsp;people depend on it.

00:07:07.075 --> 00:07:13.120
But notice that for lock guard, I have to specify this is a standard&nbsp;mutex, whereas for a scoped_block it was inferred.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.280
And scoped_lock can handle multiple locks,&nbsp;whereas lock guard can only handle one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.240
So, that's the real way that you fix these&nbsp;mangled lines in C++ 11 through 20.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:26.640
Finally, let's take a step back and think&nbsp;about the design that we have going on here.&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:27.905
I didn't really mention it before.

00:07:27.905 --> 00:07:31.764
but anytime&nbsp;that you really have two threads competing for the same resource like this,

00:07:31.764 --> 00:07:33.895
you might want&nbsp;to rethink your design.

00:07:33.895 --> 00:07:37.587
Although the mutex is protecting us from undefined behavior and mangled&nbsp;lines,

00:07:37.587 --> 00:07:40.471
it's not protecting us from bad performance.

00:07:40.471 --> 00:07:44.896
We still have two threads that are constantly&nbsp;fighting over acquiring this mutex.

00:07:44.896 --> 00:07:47.431
This is going to be horrible for performance.

00:07:47.431 --> 00:07:50.839
Instead, we may&nbsp;want to implement our own buffering where we say,

00:07:50.839 --> 00:07:53.739
write a thousand lines into a local string&nbsp;stream

00:07:53.739 --> 00:07:58.240
and then only try to grab the lock and write to the file every thousand lines.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.000
This is&nbsp;going to be much better for performance

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.075
because each thread acquires the mutex a thousand times&nbsp;less than it did before,

00:08:04.075 --> 00:08:06.531
meaning there's way less contention.

00:08:06.531 --> 00:08:09.201
And another design decision that you&nbsp;might want to think about is that

00:08:09.201 --> 00:08:15.398
it may not be the best practice to have each thread just trying&nbsp;to write to whatever file stream or standard out or whatever.

00:08:15.398 --> 00:08:19.855
In larger applications, it would be a&nbsp;common design to have a single thread

00:08:19.855 --> 00:08:23.825
in charge of all of the printing or writing to file that needs&nbsp;to be done.

00:08:23.825 --> 00:08:28.738
And other threads just submit data to the printing thread through some kind of thread&nbsp;safe queue.

